Let me analyze the vulnerability. The `memsearch_swar` function has a heap-buffer-overflow in the `memcmp` call. Let me look at the logic carefully.

The function searches for pattern `xs` (length `m`) in `ys` (length `n`).

```c
const bitint first = MASK1 * (uint8_t)xs[0];
const bitint last  = MASK1 * (uint8_t)xs[m-1];

const char *s0 = ys;
const char *s1 = ys+m-1;

const mrb_int lim = n - m - (mrb_int)sizeof(bitint);
mrb_int i;

for (i=0; i < lim; i+=sizeof(bitint)) {
    bitint t0, t1;

    memcpy(&t0, s0+i, sizeof(bitint));
    memcpy(&t1, s1+i, sizeof(bitint));

    const bitint eq = (t0 ^ first) | (t1 ^ last);
    bitint zeros = ((~eq & MASK2) + MASK1) & (~eq & MASK3);


    for (size_t j = 0; zeros; j++) {
      if (zeros & MASK4) {
        const mrb_int idx = i + j;
        const char* p = s0 + idx + 1;
        if (memcmp(p, xs + 1, m - 2) == 0) {
          return idx;
        }
      }
      ...
    }
}
```

The issue: `lim = n - m - sizeof(bitint)`. The loop runs `i` from 0 to `lim`. Inside, `idx = i + j` where `j` ranges from 0 to `sizeof(bitint)-1` (8 for 64-bit). So `idx` can be up to `lim + sizeof(bitint) - 1 = n - m - 1`. Then `p = s0 + idx + 1 = ys + idx + 1`, so `p` can be up to `ys + n - m`. Then `memcmp(p, xs+1, m-2)` reads `m-2` bytes starting at `p`, so up to `ys + n - m + m - 2 = ys + n - 2`. That's within bounds (n is the length).

Wait, but the issue is the `memcpy(&t1, s1+i, sizeof(bitint))` where `s1 = ys + m - 1`. So `s1 + i + sizeof(bitint)` can be up to `ys + m - 1 + lim + sizeof(bitint) = ys + m - 1 + n - m - sizeof(bitint) + sizeof(bitint) = ys + n - 1`. That's within bounds too.

Hmm, let me think about the actual bug. The issue says "during substring search the code can invoke memcmp past the end of the target buffer".

Let me look at the `memcmp(p, xs + 1, m - 2)` call. `p = s0 + idx + 1 = ys + idx + 1`. The memcmp reads `m-2` bytes. So it reads from `ys + idx + 1` to `ys + idx + 1 + m - 2 - 1 = ys + idx + m - 2`.

For this to be in bounds, we need `idx + m - 2 < n`, i.e., `idx < n - m + 2`.

`idx = i + j` where `i < lim = n - m - sizeof(bitint)` and `j < sizeof(bitint)`. So `idx < n - m - sizeof(bitint) + sizeof(bitint) = n - m`. So `idx < n - m`, meaning `idx + m - 2 < n - 2 < n`. So in the main loop, it's in bounds.

Wait, but there's a subtle issue. The `zeros` computation. Let me think about when `j` can be large. Actually `j` increments each iteration and `zeros` is shifted. The loop `for (size_t j = 0; zeros; j++)`. So `j` can be at most `sizeof(bitint)` (8 for 64-bit, 4 for 32-bit) because zeros gets shifted right by 8 each time. Actually after `sizeof(bitint)` shifts, zeros becomes 0. So `j` ranges 0 to `sizeof(bitint)-1`.

Hmm wait, but actually there's a real bug. Let me reconsider. The issue is the `lim` calculation can underflow. `lim = n - m - sizeof(bitint)`. If `n - m < sizeof(bitint)`, then `lim` is negative, and since `lim` is `mrb_int` (signed), the loop `i < lim` won't execute (i=0 < negative = false). So that's fine.

But wait — what if `n - m` is small but positive, like `n - m = 1`? Then `lim = 1 - 8 = -7`, loop doesn't execute. Then `i = 0`, and `i + m < n` => `0 + m < n` => `m < n` which is true (since n = m+1). So it goes to the fallback memchr path. That's fine.

